The paper catalog writes the locality once at the top of a sheet and leaves it
blank on the sheets that follow. Anyone who reads a later sheet has to assume it
belongs to the last locality written. For the specimens from Pasir Putih, that
assumption is wrong.

{% aside type="note" title="Standard museum best practice" %}
Writing the full locality on every page of a paper catalog, even when it
repeats the page before, is standard museum best practice. Each page then stands on its own if it is copied, scanned out
of order, or separated from the notebook, and a change of place is impossible
to miss. Young Heru clearly did not follow that practice: only the first sheet
carries a locality, and the Pasir Putih pages are blank.
{% /aside %}

This is the top of the sheet that holds HH 520–523. HH 520 was caught on
Line 3 at Pasir Putih, across the lake from Pondok Merah, yet the `Locality`
lines are empty:

![Paper specimen sheet with an empty Locality header above the record for HH 520, Hylomys suillus, from trap line 3](../../../../../assets/images/tutorials/basic/paper-blank-locality.jpg)

In NAHPU, a **site** is a place you enter once and reuse: a camp, a trap line, a
pitfall line. Specimens never carry the locality themselves. They link to an
event, and the event links to its site, so every specimen gets the right place
without anyone writing it again.

## 1. Plan the sites

The journal describes five lines. Line 1 mixed pitfalls and snap traps, so it
becomes two sites, one per method. Add the camp at Pondok Merah, where the team
slept, prepared specimens, and wrote the journal, and that gives seven sites.
The Site IDs follow the [NAHPU convention](/en/usages/sites/): a two-letter
locality code (`TJ` for Tujuh), then `CAMP` and its number for a camp, or the
line leader's initials, `L` for a trap line or `P` for a pitfall line, and the
line number from the paper.

| Site ID | Paper name | Area | What was set | Leader |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `TJ-CAMP1` | Not named | Pondok Merah | The camp beside the lake | Heru Handika |
| `TJHH-L1` | Line 1 | Pondok Merah | 31 snap traps | Heru Handika |
| `TJHH-P1` | Line 1 | Pondok Merah | 10 pitfalls, about 50 m from camp | Heru Handika |
| `TJBS-L2` | Line 2 | Pondok Merah | 25 snap traps, most 1–2 m above ground | Bima Sampel |
| `TJHH-L3` | Line 3 | Pasir Putih | 20 snap traps | Heru Handika |
| `TJHH-P4` | Line 4 | Pasir Putih | Pitfalls, number not recorded | Heru Handika |
| `TJHH-L5` | Line 5 | Pasir Putih | 20 snap traps, many on lianas | Heru Handika |

Keeping the paper's line numbers in the Site IDs lets anyone check a NAHPU
record against the paper form.

## 2. Create the first site

{% steps %}

1. Open Sites and select `+`.
2. Enter the Site ID `TJHH-L1`, choose `Heru Handika` as `Site Leader`, and
   choose a `Site Type` for a trap line.
3. Enter the geography from the paper header:

    | Field | Value |
    | --- | --- |
    | `Country` | `Indonesia` |
    | `State/Province` | `Jambi` |
    | `County/Parish/District` | `Kerinci` |
    | `Precise Locality` | `Gunung Tujuh, Sungai Jernih, Pondok Merah, beside Lake Gunung Tujuh` |

    The paper writes `Mt. Tujuh`. Write the full local name, *Gunung Tujuh*, so
    the locality reads the same as the gazetteer. Leave fields you cannot
    verify, such as `Municipality/City/Town`, empty.

4. Enter the habitat. The only habitat on the paper is on HH 436:
   `Mature secondary forest`. The journal for 12 September adds that the forest
   is similar along all Pondok Merah lines and slightly disturbed by hikers.

    | Field | Value |
    | --- | --- |
    | Type | `Montane forest` |
    | Condition | `Slightly disturbed` |
    | Description | `Mature secondary forest, similar along all Pondok Merah lines. Slightly disturbed by hikers.` |

    Type and condition come from lists in `Settings`; add a value there if it
    is missing.

    Habitat describes a place, not an animal. Entered here, it applies to every
    specimen from the line, not just the one specimen whose form happened to
    have it filled in.

{% /steps %}

## 3. Add the coordinate

The paper header gives one point for all of Pondok Merah:

```text
01.70274° S   101.39032° E   Elev. 2004 m
```

Before you enter it, make three corrections:

- **Sign.** Decimal degrees show the hemisphere with a sign, not a letter.
  South is negative, so the latitude is `-1.70274`. Dropping the `S` would
  move the point to the northern hemisphere, about 380 km away.
- **Unit.** On the first sheet the unit is crossed out (`feet` changed to
  `m`); the second sheet says `m asl`. NAHPU stores elevation in meters, so
  enter `2004` with no unit to argue about.
- **What is missing.** The paper has no datum and no GPS uncertainty. Do not
  invent them. Keep the default datum only if you know the GPS used WGS84, and
  leave the uncertainty empty.

{% steps %}

1. On the site page, open the coordinate panel and select `Add coordinate`.
2. Choose decimal degrees (`DD`) as the format and enter the values:

    | Field | Value |
    | --- | --- |
    | Name | `TJHH-PM` |
    | Decimal latitude | `-1.70274` |
    | Decimal longitude | `101.39032` |
    | Elevation | `2004` |
    | Notes | `One point recorded for all Pondok Merah lines in the 2014 paper catalog. Datum and uncertainty not recorded.` |

3. Select `Add`, then check the point on the map. It should sit beside Lake
   Gunung Tujuh.

{% /steps %}

The finished site, with the paper coordinate. The datum and uncertainty show
`?` because the paper did not record them:

![NAHPU Sites page on an iPad for TJHH-L1, with Indonesia, Jambi, Kerinci, the Pondok Merah locality, montane forest habitat, and coordinate TJHH-PM at -1.70274, 101.39032, 2004 m](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/site-tjhh-l1.webp)

## 4. Create the other Pondok Merah sites

{% steps %}

1. With `TJHH-L1` open, choose `Duplicate site` from the three-dot menu.
2. Enter the new Site ID `TJHH-P1` and change `Site Type` to a pitfall line.
3. Add the same coordinate again: duplicating leaves the coordinates empty.
4. Repeat for `TJBS-L2`, with `Bima Sampel` as `Site Leader`. Add
   `Most snap traps set 1–2 m above the ground.` to `Remarks`.
5. Repeat for the camp, `TJ-CAMP1`, with a camp `Site Type`. Its habitat is
   the same forest. Narratives link to this site in
   [Basic tutorial 6](/en/tutorials/basic/narrative/).

{% /steps %}

## 5. Create the Pasir Putih sites

Pasir Putih is across the lake, and the journal describes each of its three
lines differently. Create them from `+`, not by duplicating, so no Pondok Merah
value is carried over by mistake.

Use the same country, province, and county. Then enter:

| Site ID | `Precise Locality` | Condition | Description |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `TJHH-L3` | `Gunung Tujuh, Pasir Putih, across Lake Gunung Tujuh from Pondok Merah` | `Undisturbed` | `Many moss-covered trees 1–2 m in diameter. Open ground with few small plants. One rarely used trail.` |
| `TJHH-P4` | Same as `TJHH-L3` | `Undisturbed` | `Open canopy, apparently from natural processes. Dense ground cover; fewer large trees than Line 3.` |
| `TJHH-L5` | Same as `TJHH-L3` | `Undisturbed` | `Open in places and closed in others. Many lianas. Moss cover similar to Line 3.` |

Use `Montane forest` as the type for all three.

The paper has **no coordinates** for Pasir Putih. Leave the coordinate panel
empty and add this to each site's `Remarks`:
`No coordinates recorded in the 2014 paper catalog. About 1.5 hours by boat across the lake from Pondok Merah.`

{% aside type="caution" title="Do not reuse the camp coordinate" %}
It is tempting to copy the Pondok Merah point so the Pasir Putih records "have
coordinates". That would place them on the wrong side of the lake. An empty
coordinate is honest and easy to find later; a wrong one looks like good data.
If you can georeference Pasir Putih from a map, add that point with a note on
how you derived it and a realistic uncertainty.
{% /aside %}

## What you gained over paper

- Every specimen will get its locality from its site, including the ones on
  sheets with a blank header.
- Pondok Merah and Pasir Putih are separate places, so their specimens cannot
  be mixed up.
- Elevation has one unit, latitude has its sign, and missing datum and
  uncertainty are visible as empty fields, not hidden.
- Habitat is described once per place, from the journal and the one specimen
  that recorded it.

